==============================================================================
3. CAN r = 0.953 SEPARATE THE PAPER'S READING FROM SHARED ESTIMATION NOISE?
==============================================================================
  The paper quotes split-half reliabilities 0.71-0.74 (r1 DiD) and 0.69-0.72 (sts DiD).
    rel_sts=0.69, rel_r1=0.71: attenuation ceiling sqrt(rel*rel) = 0.700; observed r = 0.953 -> disattenuated 1.362
    rel_sts=0.72, rel_r1=0.74: attenuation ceiling sqrt(rel*rel) = 0.730; observed r = 0.953 -> disattenuated 1.306
    rel_sts=0.705, rel_r1=0.725: attenuation ceiling sqrt(rel*rel) = 0.715; observed r = 0.953 -> disattenuated 1.333
  => the observed collinearity EXCEEDS the ceiling two independent-noise measurements of
     perfectly correlated reliable components could reach, so a purely-reliable-signal
     account does not fit either: part of the 0.953 must be noise shared within windows.
     (The same argument the paper applies to the residual/CCS pair, Results 4.)
  Regression slope of the sts DiD on the r1 DiD, and the map slope for comparison:
    OLS slope = 4.263 nats per unit r1 (intercept -0.01844); family d sts/d r1 at (0.848, 0.24) = 5.99
    predicted group DiD from slope*mean r1 DiD = -0.06243; observed -0.08087

